#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055706} :activity/id #uuid "d981772b-339b-55c1-975c-c5ef7ec6cb31" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/id #uuid "3d5ca35e-6400-55ca-b230-0ff03628b370" :topic/math-academy-id 112} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055959 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta$, and\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta$, we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P$, and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e9912a6-a3c6-5076-9724-a53f5e215dd8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2493 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "03f7f3cb-7a44-54e1-965c-a670919e31f2" :step/math-academy-id 453943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054514 :question/id #uuid "c4d9963b-1fed-523f-89c7-961ffb12feee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b2f3c7-1600-51c6-91ad-855bcee7d18a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055445}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle Given Its Central Angle"} :step/id #uuid "38d038fc-1d12-524b-b9d7-96c6225df167" :step/math-academy-id 453944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055960 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nlet's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e7838544-050b-5270-a16a-1679e3489a02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2494 :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Unit Circle Property"} :step/id #uuid "8eef6615-9b8a-526e-9e9e-25070edfdad2" :step/math-academy-id 453948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054515 :question/id #uuid "59696ddf-7aa4-561a-ac77-2216038294b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049794}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055446}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "9d51bf5e-1d13-5f8d-9fd6-c02b852842c4" :step/math-academy-id 453949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054517 :question/id #uuid "acb093c8-daa1-5eee-8678-e985fb9dfd71" :question/math-academy-id "e-3348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8487f45b-a734-5375-abc2-5a347d49587c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055449}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "a21ae939-d688-510c-9d02-aec18c105046" :step/math-academy-id 453952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055963 :tutorial/content "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} &= \\frac{y}{z} \\div \\frac{x}{z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y}{z} \\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y \\cdot z}{x \\cdot z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y \\cdot z}{x \\cdot z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y}{x} \\\\\n&= \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta}\n$$\n\nit immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a17ae159-de66-547d-8e9a-2190b512ab3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2725 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between Sine, Cosine, and Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "68735c4a-f7f9-5265-80af-ded103195f00" :step/math-academy-id 453955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054516 :question/id #uuid "ee188df6-0ef5-5819-8fd9-77d48e8a5dce" :question/math-academy-id "e-3347"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a4297b-016b-5182-bf4f-cd2b64a23c41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055448}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent or Cotangent of a Central Angle Given a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "8e354a30-502e-5775-810b-ba93046cedfb" :step/math-academy-id 453956}] :activity/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054870}]}
